  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem requires finding a function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral over shifts. Previous strategies used greedy local search around a parent solution. This new approach explores the solution space using a diffusion-inspired method: represent the function as a probability distribution and optimize by evolving probability states. The key improvements are:\n1. Using probability distributions ensures proper balance between 0s and 1s\n2. Maintaining sum constraint precisely by design\n3. Using Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) with adaptive learning rates\n4. Incorporating heat diffusion dynamics initially to escape local optima\n5. Gradually increasing step sizes to find the global minimum\n\nThe autor acceleration any valid h_values by maintaining the exact sum of the pieces. We use gradient-based optimization, which is efficient for continuous variables.\n\nMathematical specification:\nRepresentation:\n- Probability distribution p: \u211d \u2192 [0,1] with \u222b\u2080\u00b2 p(x) dx = 1\n- The function h is a discretization of p over n_points samples\n\nVariables:\n- p(x) for x \u2208 [0,2] represented by samples at points x_i\n- The samples must be in [0,1] and sum to 1 (after scaling by dx=2.0/n_points)\n\nObjective:\n_minimize max over k of \u222b\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over the overlap regions\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i] * dx = 1 exactly\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Precompute initial probability distribution from parent solution\n2. Scale to ensure sum constraint if needed\n3. Use warm-start optimization with SGD\n- Define loss function L(h) = max_k scaled_convolution\n- Compute gradient with finite differences\n- Use adaptive learning rate annealing\n- Employ specialized feasibility maintenance:\n  - Clamping for boundary values\n  - Iterative rebalancing for sum constraint\n\nSteps:\n1. Initialize h from parent solution, ensuring sum constraint\n2. Use stochastic gradient descent with:\n   - Weight clipping (0 to 1)\n   - Iterative sum constraint repair\n   - Temperature parameter controlling update size\n3. Track best solution across iterations\n\nSearch choices:\n- Temperature starts high (1.0) and decreases exponentially\n- Learning rate starts at 0.1 and halves when loss plateaus\n- Number of iterations per n_points: 1000\n\nValidation:\n- After each iteration, reproject to feasible set\n- Re-evaluate objective with original overlap method\n- Clip to bounds until feasible solutions dominate\n\nFeasibility restoration:\n- After gradient step, repair sum constraint by redistributing delta\namong samples\n- For extreme values, clamp and rebalance\n\nReturn contract:\n- Always return tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Maintain sum_sample = (n_points/2) / dx exactly\n- Use provided initial_h_values if available\n\nError handling:\n- If gradient diverges, reduce step size\n- If loss plateau, inject restart candidate\n- If time budget tight, skip initial convolution",
